'''''Symphyotrichum subulatum''''' (formerly ''Aster subulatus''), commonly known as '''eastern annual saltmarsh aster''' or, in Britain and Ireland where it is naturalized, '''annual saltmarsh aster''',{{Sfn|BSBI|2007}} is an [[annual plant|annual]] plant in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Asteraceae]] native to the eastern United States and the [[Gulf Coast of the United States|Gulf Coast]] to [[Texas]].{{sfn|USDA|2021}} The species grows primarily in coastal [[salt marsh]]es, although in the [[Ozarks]] it occurs as a non-marine weedy variety.{{sfn|Gleason|Cronquist|1991}}

==Description==
''Symphyotrichum subulatum'' is an [[Annual plant|annual]] forb that possesses a single erect stem that can reach up to {{convert|1|m|ftin|0|sp=us|abbr=off}} in height. The stem, along with its thin green to dark green leaves, are both hairless. The sheathing base-blades of the leaves are ovulate, and the margins are [[Entire (botany)|entire]].{{sfn|Sharma|Bhattacharjee|Lakshminarasimhan|2012}}

The top of the stem extends into a [[raceme]] [[inflorescence]]. The heads open up into bright yellow [[disc floret]]s that are surrounded by [[ray floret]]s that vary in color from white to lavender.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}
<gallery>
Aster subulatus heads1 (11972029663).jpg|''S. subulatum''
Symphyotrichum subulatum 114887428.jpg|''S. subulatum'' base
Symphyotrichum subulatum var sandwicensis leaves.jpg|''S. subulatum'' stem and leaves
</gallery>

==Taxonomy==
The species' full scientific name is ''Symphyotrichum subulatum'' <small>(Michx.) G.L.Nesom</small>. {{As of|2021|December}}, three varieties of ''S.&nbsp;subulatum'' were accepted by [[Plants of the World Online]] (POWO), with ''S.&nbsp;subulatum'' var. ''subulatum'' as the [[Autonym (botany)|autonym]]:{{sfn|POWO|2021a}}
*''S. subulatum'' var. ''subulatum''{{sfn|POWO|2021d}}
*''S. subulatum'' var. ''elongatum'' <small>(Boss. ex A.G.Jones & Lowry) S.D.Sundb.</small>{{sfn|POWO|2021e}}
*''S. subulatum'' var. ''squamatum'' <small>(Spreng.) S.D.Sundb.</small>{{sfn|POWO|2021f}}

The varieties ''S.&nbsp;subulatum'' var. ''ligulatum'' <small>(Shinners) S.D.Sundb.</small> and ''S.&nbsp;s.'' var. ''parviflorum'' <small>(Nees) S.D.Sundb.</small>, {{As of|2021|December|lc=yes}}, are accepted at the species level by POWO as ''[[Symphyotrichum divaricatum]]'' <small>(Nutt.) G.L.Nesom</small>{{sfn|POWO|2021b}} and ''Symphyotrichum expansum'' <small>(Poepp. ex Spreng.) G.L.Nesom</small>,{{sfn|POWO|2021c}} respectively. {{As of|2021|December}}, ''S.&nbsp;s.'' var. ''parviflorum'' <small>(Nees) S.D.Sundb.</small> is accepted at the species level by [[Catalogue of Life]] (COL) as ''[[Symphyotrichum parviflorum]]''.{{Sfn|Hassler|2021}}

==Distribution and habitat==
''Symphyotrichum subulatum'' is a terrestrial species that will typically grow among grasses of any kind.{{sfn|Sharma|Bhattacharjee|Lakshminarasimhan|2012}} It is found in salt marshes, pond margins, sloughs, swamps, crop field margins, lawns, and roadsides. It is thought to be especially prevalent in these areas because of a tolerance to saline soils and mowing.{{sfn|Missouri Native Plant Society|2018}} It is native to the eastern and Gulf Coast areas of the United States. It is also common across Mexico, the West Indies, Bermuda, Central America, and South America, depending on variety.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}

==Uses==
According to the [[Lady Bird Johnson Wildflower Center]], eastern annual saltmarsh aster can be planted to attract butterflies and has value for native bees.{{sfn|TWC Staff|2018}}

